# plans/board-workflows-2026-09-20-phase-9-plan.md P9-T05 · KEYMAP.md §Board and card ·
# UX-SPEC.md §Board ("Keyboard") · TESTING-HARNESS.md §3 (`card.runs`, `dialog.message`) — the
# five keys phase 9 gives a card's *run*, end to end against a real daemon and real children.
#
# What each step pins, and why it is the shape it is:
#   `A` twice     the refusal first (`AGE-1 has no run`, contracts §5.5) and then the attach:
#                 the run's thread is an ordinary agent tab titled `↳ AGE-1 — …`, and `A`
#                 selects it with the keyboard in its composer, exactly as a delegated child is
#                 selected. It is a *selection* rather than an attachment because a card-called
#                 child has no caller thread and so is never hidden to begin with
#                 (`state::agents::is_attached`): its tab joins the strip when the run starts.
#   `^s u`        the way back. A card is a caller like a thread is, so the key that returns a
#                 child to its caller returns this one to the board tab with its card focused.
#   `^s d`        the AGENTS picker lists the run at *top level* — `badges[1] == caller` — and
#                 labels it with the same tab title. That is the no-work claim of the phase: a
#                 card-called child has `parent: None` (`delegation/run.rs`), so every list that
#                 groups children under their caller thread already treats it as its own root.
#   `>` twice     the refusal in a column that runs nothing (`Ready has no action`, asked of
#                 AGE-1 once its cancelled run has put it back in Ready — a card moved into
#                 Ready with nothing blocking it would otherwise go straight on, §11.7 rule 0)
#                 and the re-run ask on a card that has runs.
#   `X`           the confirm, its sentence, and the run reaching a terminal outcome: the first
#                 row of `card.runs` reads `cancelled …` and stays that way.
#   `]` and `[`   `ConfirmRequest::MoveCancelsRun`, read back through `dialog.message` — the one
#                 sentence a user confirms (§3.8.3). `[` is accepted on AGE-1 and `]` refused
#                 with `n` on AGE-2, each in its own run's window, for the reason below.
#
# The board is the `board-workflow` preset's (§4): seven workflow columns, `maxLiveRuns` of 1,
# and four cards in Todo whose links — AGE-1 blocks AGE-3, AGE-1 and AGE-2 block AGE-4 — are the
# chain this file drives. The plan asked for that chain to be built by a scripted thread's
# `shell` steps running `fleet board card new --blocked-by` and `card move`; the preset has
# seeded exactly those links since P6-T05, and §9.2 is explicit that a scenario must not build
# with lines what a preset already seeds. So the chain is read off the face and only the moves
# are driven. The phase tracker records the deviation.
#
# Three limits shape the order everything below is done in, all three measured rather than
# assumed (P6-T05, and this file's own first runs):
#   * a scripted child parks on its permission gate for `agent::GATE_BUDGET` (5 s) and is then
#     torn down, which ends its run. A run mark is therefore a state that arrives rather than one
#     the board is already in, and every predicate about one here is an `await`, never an
#     `assert`. (Until 2026-09-21 the column then started the card again, immediately and for
#     ever; that was a defect and is fixed — a terminal run now leaves its card alone.)
#   * one live run at a time. A second card is given a run once the first has ended or *left*
#     its action column, and the accepted `[` below is the second of those: it is the move
#     confirm and the way the board is quietened while a child is still out there.
#   * the move confirm is asked only while a live card-called child stands behind the card's
#     newest run (contracts §5.5); with none the plain move goes out and the daemon's `{KEY} is
#     working; pass --cancel-run to move it` lands in the sticky slot instead. So each `[` / `]`
#     here waits for the *delegation* to say `blocked` first: that is the child at its gate, the
#     beginning of the twenty seconds it holds one, and the widest window a scenario can start a
#     keystroke in. Waiting on the tile's `working` instead is what an earlier draft did, and it
#     is not the same claim: that mark is also satisfied by the board view's own `live_runs`
#     join, before any child exists to cancel. For the same reason the two confirms are in two
#     windows — the accepted `[` on AGE-1, the refused `]` on AGE-2 — rather than back to back
#     in one.
# The consequence for `>`: now that a terminal run does not restart its own column, the key is
# the only thing that can put a second run on a card standing still — so the second row below is
# `>`'s own. What is pinned here is that the key reaches the daemon, that it refuses in the
# column's own words where there is nothing to run, and that a second run stands on the card
# afterwards; the sentence it refuses with is `screens::board::runs`' unit tests' half.
#
# No `shot`: this is a keyboard scenario, so it runs in the headless lane too (§7).
fixture: board-workflow
await idle 30000

# The preset seeds two worktrees, and `agent` is the one carrying the board.
assert lists.worktrees.rows[1].id == "acme/api#agent"
key j
await lists.worktrees.selected.id == "acme/api#agent" 10000
key o
await screen == Workspace 30000
key ctrl-s b
await lists.tabs.selected.label == "board" 30000
await focused == "board.column[0].card[0]" 30000
await key_contexts[2] == "Board" 20000

# Todo is the one column the preset leaves human, and the chain is on its face: AGE-1 blocks
# AGE-3, and AGE-1 and AGE-2 together block AGE-4.
key l
await lists.board.selected.label == "Todo" 10000
assert lists["board.cards"].rows[0].badges[0] == "AGE-1" && lists["board.cards"].rows[0].marks[0] absent
assert lists["board.cards"].rows[1].badges[0] == "AGE-2" && lists["board.cards"].rows[1].marks[0] absent
assert lists["board.cards"].rows[2].badges[0] == "AGE-3" && lists["board.cards"].rows[2].marks[0] == "blocked:1"
assert lists["board.cards"].rows[3].badges[0] == "AGE-4" && lists["board.cards"].rows[3].marks[0] == "blocked:2"

# `A` on a card that has never run says so and does nothing else. A refusal is a sentence, not
# silence (contracts §5.5).
key shift-a
await toasts[0].text == "AGE-1 has no run" 15000
advance 20000
await toasts[0] absent 10000

# Into Ready, which routes a card nothing blocks straight on (BOARD.md §11.7 rule 0): one `]`
# lands AGE-1 in In Progress, the prompt column, and the cursor follows it there. The card's
# default provider is Claude, whose child asks a question and parks on it. The tile is painted
# from the delegation mirror (BOARD.md §11.8), so a child that opens its gate milliseconds after
# its turn starts reaches the face as `needs you` without ever showing `working`; the delegation
# itself says `blocked` once the gate is open — which is what gives `[` a live child to offer to
# cancel, and the beginning of the twenty seconds (`agent::GATE_BUDGET`) the child holds it for.
key ]
await lists.board.selected.label == "In Progress" 20000
await lists["board.cards"].rows[0].marks[0] == "needs you" 60000
await agents.delegations[0].status == blocked 90000

# `[` on a card whose child is out there: the move and the cancellation are one sentence,
# answered once. Accepted, so AGE-1 lands in a column that runs nothing — which is what frees
# the board's one run slot for the next card in the chain.
key [
await dialog.name == Confirm 15000
assert dialog.message ~= "^AGE-1 is working \(.+\)\. Move to Ready and cancel the run\?$"
key y
await lists.board.selected.label == "Ready" 30000
await lists["board.cards"].rows[0].badges[0] == "AGE-1" 15000

# Ready runs nothing: `>` names the column rather than starting something no automation asked
# for. (The card stays in Ready: rule 0 skips a card whose cancelled run is still live, and the
# cancel's delivery settles the card where it stands.) The key reads the column of a card's live
# run, so it waits for the cancel to be delivered before it asks.
await agents.delegations[0].status == cancelled 30000
await lists["board.cards"].rows[0].marks[0] absent 15000
await idle 30000
key >
await toasts[0].text == "Ready has no action" 15000
advance 20000
await toasts[0] absent 10000

# `A` — the run as an ordinary tab. A run that has ended still has its thread, and the tab it
# opens is the ordinary one: the thread title the daemon minted is `↳ AGE-1 — …`
# (`delegation/footer.rs::card_child_title`), which is what the strip carries, with the provider
# as the badge beside it.
key shift-a
await lists.tabs.selected.label ~= "^↳ AGE-1 — " 30000
assert lists.tabs.selected.badges[0] == claude
assert mode == Agent && focused == agents.composer

# `^s u` — the way back the card caller's pinned segment also offers: the board tab, with the
# card the run worked for focused, in the column it is in now.
key ctrl-s u
await lists.tabs.selected.label == "board" 30000
await focused == "board.column[2].card[0]" 20000
assert key_contexts[2] == "Board"

# `^s d` — the run is its own root in the AGENTS picker, labelled by its tab title. Every row
# here is a run of AGE-1, because it is the only card that has run.
key ctrl-s d
await lists.palette.rows[0].label ~= "^claude — ↳ AGE-1 — " 15000
assert lists.palette.rows[0].badges[0] == claude
assert lists.palette.rows[0].badges[1] == caller
assert lists.palette.rows[0].badges[2] == "acme/api#agent"
assert lists.palette.rows[0].marks[1] == go
key escape
await overlay absent 10000
await focused == "board.column[2].card[0]" 15000

# The next card in the chain, through Ready into the column that runs it. Nothing is running
# now, so this card is given the slot rather than owed it, and one `]` carries it through.
key h
await lists.board.selected.label == "Todo" 15000
assert lists["board.cards"].rows[0].badges[0] == "AGE-2"
key ]
await lists.board.selected.label == "In Progress" 20000
assert lists["board.cards"].rows[0].badges[0] == "AGE-2"
await lists["board.cards"].rows[0].marks[0] == "needs you" 90000
await agents.delegations[1].status == blocked 90000

# `]` on this one, and refused: the same confirm, naming the column the move would land in, and
# `n` leaves the card and its child exactly where they are.
key ]
await dialog.name == Confirm 15000
assert dialog.message ~= "^AGE-2 is working \(.+\)\. Move to In review and cancel the run\?$"
key n
await dialog absent 10000
await focused == "board.column[3].card[0]" 15000
assert lists.board.selected.label == "In Progress"

# `X` — the confirm states what stops, and only the accepted dialog reaches the wire. This one
# asks nothing of the delegation mirror: the card is the authority on whether it has a run.
key shift-x
await dialog.name == Confirm 15000
assert dialog.message == "Stops the run. What it already changed in the worktree is kept."
key y
await dialog absent 20000

# The run the `y` stopped is terminal, and the detail is where a card's runs are read: the
# oldest row is the cancelled one and stays so, whatever the column starts next.
key enter
await dialog.name == CardDetail 15000
await lists["card.runs"].rows[0].label ~= "^cancelled " 90000
assert lists["card.runs"].rows[0].badges[0] == "claude"
dump runs

# `>` from the detail asks the column to run the card again — and nothing else can, now that a
# run ending where it started leaves its card alone, so the second row is this key's own work.
key >
await lists["card.runs"].rows[1] exists 90000
key escape
await dialog absent 10000
await focused == "board.column[3].card[0]" 15000
assert lists["board.cards"].rows[0].badges[0] == "AGE-2"
quit
